  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0d/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_49.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Angel takes a shortcut over the factory of Tony Stark, but he sees Iron Man below, waving at him.&nbsp; A deadly explosion is about to be tested, and Iron Man is trying to warn him off, but it's too late - the explosion rocks the scene, and while Iron Man has his armor to protect him, the Angel is caught full with the blast, and the ensuing radiation changes him - turns him evil, and he takes off!&nbsp; Iron Man sees the change and pursues, but his boot jets run out, and he has to make an emergency landing, allowing the Angel to escape.\n\nSoon, at the X-Men HQ, Angel announces that he's quitting the team!&nbsp; He's going to go find the evil mutants and join them.&nbsp; The other X-Men try, but fail to stop him.&nbsp; When Professor X returns, he admits the situation is serious enough to call the Avengers for help.&nbsp; But the Hulk, Thor, Giant-Man and the Wasp are all occupied, and only Iron Man receives the call.&nbsp; He feels responsible for the Angel's turn and decides to handle this by himself.\n\nAngel is out hunting for the evil mutants, making a ruckus so that he gets noticed by them.&nbsp; But \"the wary evil mutants, recognizing the Angel, suspect a <i>trap</i> and do <i>not</i> show themselves!\"&nbsp; But Iron Man heads for the scene.&nbsp; The two scuffle and evade each other, Iron Man not fully appreciating the extent of Angel's personality shift.&nbsp; He still thinks that somehow Angel hasn't fully turned bad, so he shoots toward him and grabs, pulling them both as high as his boot jets last.&nbsp; They eventually sputter out, and Iron Man begins to fall.&nbsp; He's now completely at Angel's mercy.\n\nIron Man falls to his doom and realizes there's nothing that can be done to save him.&nbsp; Angel stares at him, knowing he has no chance.&nbsp; He can't do it!&nbsp; He shoots down and rescues Iron Man, who has now proven that he's <i>not</i> evil.&nbsp; The shock of what happened finally opened the Angel's eye as to what happened - the atomic rays \"affected his brain for a time...until <i>now</i>!\"&nbsp; Iron Man risked his life to shock him back to normal.\n\nThe other X-Men arrive on the scene to take Angel home, and the Professor mentally reaches out to thank Iron Man for risking his life for one of his X-Men and offers his help, should he ever need it.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "Considering how many times this tale was referenced over the years (they even reprinted it in a GS or Annual), to finally read it was something I had never done.&nbsp; <i>Meh</i> is about the only word to describe it.&nbsp; The personality-altering plot device was just as uninspired here as it was in the previous <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> issue that put Thor side-by-side with Loki.&nbsp; It was nice seeing the characters crossover of course - seeing that the X-Men and Avengers are all around and could appear at any moment in each other's books.&nbsp; But the story did little to elevate itself above the \"reader\" level (though see note below).\n\nIron Man's boot jets and power levels are still a pale imitation of what they eventually will become - he runs out of power far too many times, but obviously his armor is constantly being upgraded.&nbsp; At this point, he can hardly be considered the second most powerful Avenger, as he later becomes, second only to Thor.&nbsp; (Refer to <i>Iron Man</i> #114 for a fantastic statement to this effect - also Avengers Annual 8.)\n\nHeh, it's amazing how Happy and Pepper are obviously crazy about each other, but Pepper in particular hides this by constantly hurling insults in Happy's direction.\n\nNote:&nbsp; at the bottom of page 8, we see that Hank Pym and Janet Van Dyne are at a \"rare social evening together\" and \"only have eyes and thoughts for each other!\"&nbsp; Hmm, that's a completely different impression than what we get in the various <i>Tales to Astonish</i> issues - Hank has been trying to keep things cool between them.&nbsp; Also, as of this point, it seems the Hulk is still listed as a member of the Avengers, even though he quit at the end of the last published issue.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMarvel obviously felt they had some major copyright issues to deal with (<i>wink wink</i>), since the X-Men and the Avengers appear courtesy of a \"special arrangement\" by the publishers of those magazines, and then we turn the page to the backup feature, only to find the Watcher appearing thanks to the Fantastic Four magazine.&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>, there are guest stars all over the place, and other magazines seemingly waiting in line to share the profits!\n\n\n\nThe Watcher's use as narrator in the <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> backup tale called \"The Saga of the Sneepers\" is notable enough to add an extra half-point to the final rating of the book, not because he actually <i>does</i> anything in the book (because he doesn't), but because he's able to share information with the reader about events transpiring elsewhere.&nbsp; His use, along with his unique perspective is worthy of a [SPAN: severity=success; text=one-time bonus] -- in this case, half a point.",
  "side_note": [
    "[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=Tales of the Watcher]\n\n[ASSET: left | TalesOfTheWatcher | width=20%] In this brief feature, the Watcher makes only his third appearance (after [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13|2026-07-04] and [LINK:1963:FF_20|FF #20|2026-12-25]) and serves as narrator, in this case introducing readers to the \"Sneepers,\" a conquering race which is keeping a close eye on Earth at a distance, concerned that earthlings continue to make advancement after advancement.&nbsp; They wonder if earthlings will eventually become strong enough to conquer <i>them</i> instead of vice versa.\n[BR]\n[BR] In the end, after observing that the people of Earth appear to be threatening each <b><i>other</i></b>, they elect to do nothing at all!\n\nThe Watcher then addresses the reader directly and points out that \"the Sneepers wait for your self-destruction!&nbsp; But you must <i>not</i> allow it to happen!\"&nbsp; He goes on to tell readers that only <i>they</i> have the power to prove the Sneepers wrong -- [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=\"You must heed my warning!\"]\n\nAs for him, he can only observe -- he has no power to <i>act</i>... \"for I am... <b><i>the Watcher</i></b>!\"\n\nUnquestionably, the ending lands a bit on the [SPAN: severity=info; text=preachy side], but the direct callout by the Watcher gives Stan a veritable soapbox upon which he can leap to make sweeping observations about the state of humanity.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And <i>nowhere</i> is this more pronounced than in the still-to-come <b><i>Silver Surfer</i></b> title of the later 1960s!]\n\nBut rather than elaborate more on this as part of one issue's review, I've elected to hold back most of my commentary until this month's summary (which is next in the reading order), so check that out."
  ],
